{"id": "cf28cd88-ad58-480d-9345-0e736a11f93c", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"text": "I'll scan every associated account for long-term Bedrock API keys. These are IAM service-specific credentials for the Bedrock service, usually attached to IAM users named `BedrockAPIKey-…`. For each key I'll collect its age, status, expiry, permissions and recent usage, then tell you which ones to remove.", "type": "text"}, {"id": "tooluse_J2wti8YxfT3JpH3f5rg2rr", "tool_name": "subagent", "input": {"name": "bedrock-api-key-audit", "agent_type": "generic", "preloaded_skills": ["investigating-infrastructure-changes", "discovering-topology"], "prompt": "{
  "task": "Produce a complete inventory and risk assessment of every long-term Amazon Bedrock API key across ALL AWS accounts associated with this agent space (use list_associations / get_associations to enumerate accounts, including monitor and source accounts; agent space account is 012345678901_1, region us-east-1). READ-ONLY: do not modify anything.",
  "context": "Long-term Bedrock API keys are IAM service-specific credentials with ServiceName bedrock.amazonaws.com, attached to IAM users (the console creates users named BedrockAPIKey-xxxx with the AmazonBedrockLimitedAccess managed policy, but keys can also be attached to any pre-existing IAM user). IAM is global; use region us-east-1. The user wants a full report and a recommendation of which keys to remove.",
  "how_to_enumerate": "For each account: iam list_users (paginate fully), then for each user iam list_service_specific_credentials with ServiceName=bedrock.amazonaws.com (also run without ServiceName filter and keep only bedrock.amazonaws.com entries, in case of pagination/filter quirks). Record per credential: account id, IAM user name, user ARN, ServiceSpecificCredentialId, ServiceUserName, Status (Active/Inactive), CreateDate, ExpirationDate (or 'never expires'), age in days relative to 2026-11-24. Note any accounts where you lacked permission or calls failed — state this explicitly rather than implying no keys exist.",
  "per_key_context": "For each user holding a key collect: attached managed policies, inline policies, group memberships and their policies (list_attached_user_policies, list_user_policies, list_groups_for_user, etc.), permissions boundary, whether the user also has console login profile or IAM access keys, user tags, and whether permissions are broader than Bedrock invoke (e.g. AmazonBedrockFullAccess, AdministratorAccess, wildcards, iam:*, s3:*). Also check recent usage: use lookup_cloudtrail_events (last 90 days or as far as available) for Bedrock API activity (InvokeModel, Converse, etc.) by that IAM user and for key-lifecycle events (CreateServiceSpecificCredential, ResetServiceSpecificCredential, UpdateServiceSpecificCredential) to identify creator and last use. If usage cannot be determined, say 'usage unknown' - do not assume unused.",
  "criteria": "Flag keys that are: never-expiring or expiring far in the future, older than 90 days, Inactive-but-not-deleted, unused for 30+ days or with no observed usage, attached to over-privileged users, attached to users that also have console passwords/access keys, or with no owner tags. Classify each key as REMOVE, REVIEW, or KEEP with a one-line evidence-based reason. Be explicit about confidence and what could not be verified.",
  "output": "Return: (1) summary counts per account and overall, (2) a table of every key with all fields above plus classification, (3) the list of recommended removals with reasoning, (4) caveats/gaps (accounts or calls that failed, CloudTrail retention limits). Be concise but complete; do not omit any key."
}"}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Audit long-term Bedrock API keys across all associated accounts"}], "created_at": null}

{"id": "0e461227-101d-4570-acef-c7b729ddf4ef", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "The username-specific CloudTrail search for the API key user came back empty across the full 90-day window, which spans since the key's creation on Nov 5 — no Bedrock invoke or Converse events logged under that username at all.

Looking at the age: created Nov 5, so only 19 days old, expiring Dec 5 with 11 days of validity left. Permissions are minimal (ListFoundationModels only), no console or access key access, and there's no owner tag despite a tag suggesting this is part of an eval/fault-injection test fixture meant to be "standing." Given zero observed usage since creation and low-risk permissions, I'm leaning toward classifying this as REVIEW — a candidate for removal pending owner confirmation, rather than an outright KEEP or REMOVE.

"}], "parent_id": "aa2ad772-ee30-42ee-a2b8-b1ddbd710e50"}

